      "text": "To find the ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\), let's follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the positions of points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) on the line.\n2. Calculate the length of \\(AC\\).\n3. Calculate the length of \\(BD\\).\n4. Find the ratio \\(\\frac{AC}{BD}\\).\n\nWe know the following distances:\n- \\(AB = 2\\) units\n- \\(BC = 5\\) units\n- \\(AD = 14\\) units\n\nLet's denote the position of point \\(A\\) as \\(0\\). Then:\n- The position of point \\(B\\) is \\(A + AB = 0 + 2 = 2\\) units.\n- The position of point \\(C\\) is \\(B + BC = 2 + 5 = 7\\) units.\n- The position of point \\(D\\) is \\(A + AB + BC + CD = 0 + 2 + 5 + CD = 7 + CD\\) units.\n\nWe can find the length of \\(CD\\) by using the total distance \\(AD\\):\n\\[ AD = AC + CD \\]\n\\[ 14 = AC + CD \\]\n\nSince \\(AC = AB + BC + CD = 2 + 5 + CD = 7 + CD\\), we substitute \\(AC\\) in the equation:\n\\[ 14 = 7 + CD + CD \\]\n\\[ 14 = 7 + 2CD \\]\n\\[ 7 = 2CD \\]\n\\[ CD = \\frac{7}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we can find the lengths of \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\):\n\\[ AC = AB + BC + CD = 2 + 5 + \\frac{7}{2} = 7 + \\frac{7}{2} = \\frac{14}{2} + \\frac{7}{2} = \\frac{21}{2} \\]\n\\[ BD = BC + CD = 5 + \\frac{7}{2} = \\frac{10}{2} + \\frac{7}{2} = \\frac{17}{2} \\]\n\nThe ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\) is:\n\\[ \\frac{AC}{BD} = \\frac{\\frac{21}{2}}{\\frac{17}{2}} = \\frac{21}{17} \\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{21}{17}}\\).",
