      "text": "To solve for the ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\), we first need to determine the lengths of \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(AB = 2\\) units\n- \\(BC = 5\\) units\n- \\(AD = 14\\) units\n\nSince points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) are in that order on a line, we can express the lengths in terms of the positions of these points. Let \\(A\\) be at position \\(0\\). Then:\n- \\(B\\) is at position \\(2\\)\n- \\(C\\) is at position \\(2 + 5 = 7\\)\n- \\(D\\) is at position \\(0 + 14 = 14\\)\n\nNow, we can find the lengths \\(AC\\) and \\(BD\\):\n- \\(AC = C - A = 7 - 0 = 7\\) units\n- \\(BD = D - B = 14 - 2 = 12\\) units\n\nThe ratio of \\(AC\\) to \\(BD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{AC}{BD} = \\frac{7}{12}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{12}}\n\\]",
